15 0 obj
<< /A 40 0 R /Alt (Infographic summarizing a longitudinal Chinese study linking higher long-term exposure to air pollutants \(PM1, PM2.5, PM10, O3, NO2\) with increased cardiovascular disease risk in older adults with cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic syndrome stages 0-3. Includes statistical results, mediation analysis, dose-response graphs, and illustrations of cardiovascular outcomes including stroke, heart attack, angina, and heart failure. Key hazard ratios and mediation effects are highlighted, with PM10 showing the strongest association in standardized sensitivity analysis.) /K 2 /P 8 0 R /Pg 27 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Panel A presents a timeline diagram of four study waves in 2011, 2013, 2015, and 2018, listing milestones such as participant recruitment, baseline information, air pollution assessment for specific years, and cardiovascular disease \(CVD\) status. Panel B is a flowchart detailing participant inclusion and exclusion from the 2011 CHARLS baseline through to the final analysis, showing sequential exclusion of participants based on age, CKM syndrome status, missing CVD data, follow-up loss, and air pollution data, resulting in a final sample of 7,400 participants.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 29 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Forest plot graphic showing hazard ratios with 95 percent confidence intervals for five air pollutants: PM1, PM2.5, PM10, O3, and NO2. PM1, PM2.5, PM10, and NO2 are statistically significant risk factors with hazard ratios above one, while O3 is not statistically significant as its confidence interval includes one. All hazardous pollutants except O3 show significant association with risk according to p-values noted below the plot.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Five line graphs labeled A through E display the hazard ratio of incident cardiovascular disease versus average annual exposure to different air pollutants \(PM1, PM2.5, PM10, O3, and NO2\). Each graph includes a reference line at hazard ratio one, a median exposure value marked by a vertical line, and shaded confidence intervals. Most graphs show a nonlinear increase in cardiovascular risk at higher pollutant concentrations, with statistical significance indicated for all but graph D \(O3\).   Graph A: PM1 shows a U-shaped association. Graph B: PM2.5 presents a similar U-shaped trend. Graph C: PM10 shows a monotonic upward trend. Graph D: O3 demonstrates minimal association. Graph E: NO2 displays a U-shaped pattern.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Grouped panel of twelve error bar charts showing hazard ratios and 95 percent confidence intervals for cardiovascular diseases, heart disease, and stroke across age, sex, residence, and education levels by air pollutant exposure \(PM1, PM2.5, PM10, O3, NO2\). Each subgroup is represented with separate colored markers, with asterisks indicating statistically significant associations for some groups.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
